{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0152C7Jf3Lq6sFzmBkv6DHKG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n4\t//!   * `hsv_to_rgb`\n5\t//!\n6\t//! The translation is intentionally literal: operation order, `f32` arithmetic,\n7\t//! the `(int)floorf(h)` truncation and the `switch` fall-through structure are\n8\t//! reproduced exactly so that the output is bit-identical to the C version.\n9\t\n10\tuse std::ffi::c_float;\n11\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n12\t\n13\t/// Reproduces the target machine's native `float` -> `int` truncating\n14\t/// conversion, matching what the C compiler emits for `(int)expr`.\n15\t///\n16\t/// Rust's `as` cast *saturates* (NaN -> 0, out-of-range -> `i32::MIN`/`i32::MAX`),\n17\t/// but C leaves the out-of-range case undefined, so the observable behaviour of\n18\t/// the C shared object is whatever the hardware does. On x86-64 the compiler\n19\t/// emits `cvttss2si`, which raises #IA and returns the \"integer indefinite\"\n20\t/// value `0x8000_0000` for NaN *and* for any value outside `[-2^31, 2^31)`.\n21\t/// Faithfully mirroring that is required for bit-identical output (e.g. a NaN\n22\t/// hue must select the `switch` `default` arm, not `case 0`).\n23\t#[inline]\n24\t#[cfg(target_arch = \"x86_64\")]\n25\tfn c_float_to_int(x: c_float) -> c_int {\n26\t    if x.is_nan() || x >= 2_147_483_648.0f32 || x <= -2_147_483_648.0f32 {\n27\t        c_int::MIN\n28\t    } else {\n29\t        x as c_int\n30\t    }\n31\t}\n32\t\n33\t/// Non-x86-64 fallback: AArch64's `fcvtzs` (and most other ISAs) clamp exactly\n34\t/// the way Rust's saturating `as` cast does, so the plain cast is already the\n35\t/// faithful translation there.\n36\t#[inline]\n37\t#[cfg(not(target_arch = \"x86_64\"))]\n38\tfn c_float_to_int(x: c_float) -> c_int {\n39\t    x as c_int\n40\t}\n41\t\n42\t/// Turn a NaN into its quiet form the way the hardware does when it propagates\n43\t/// an operand: the quiet bit is set, every other payload bit is preserved.\n44\t#[inline]\n45\tfn quiet(x: c_float) -> c_float {\n46\t    c_float::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n47\t}\n48\t\n49\t/// `subss dest, src` — i.e. `dest - src` with x86 NaN-operand selection.\n50\t///\n51\t/// The three arithmetic helpers below exist because a NaN *payload* is\n52\t/// observable in this API's output, and which payload survives depends on which\n53\t/// operand of the SSE instruction is the destination (Intel SDM \"Rules for\n54\t/// handling NaNs\": the first/destination operand wins if it is a NaN, otherwise\n55\t/// the second one does, and an SNaN is quieted on the way out). Plain Rust `-`\n56\t/// and `*` leave that choice to LLVM's register allocator, which does *not*\n57\t/// always agree with gcc's, so the order is pinned here instead. Every ordering\n58\t/// was read off `objdump -d` of the C `.so` and is identical for gcc at -O0,\n59\t/// -O1, -O2, -O3 and -Os.\n60\t#[inline]\n61\tfn subss(dest: c_float, src: c_float) -> c_float {\n62\t    if dest.is_nan() {\n63\t        quiet(dest)\n64\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n65\t        quiet(src)\n66\t    } else {\n67\t        dest - src\n68\t    }\n69\t}\n70\t\n71\t/// `mulss dest, src` — i.e. `dest * src` with x86 NaN-operand selection.\n72\t#[inline]\n73\tfn mulss(dest: c_float, src: c_float) -> c_float {\n74\t    if dest.is_nan() {\n75\t        quiet(dest)\n76\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n77\t        quiet(src)\n78\t    } else {\n79\t        dest * src\n80\t    }\n81\t}\n82\t\n83\t/// `divss dest, src` — i.e. `dest / src` with x86 NaN-operand selection.\n84\t#[inline]\n85\tfn divss(dest: c_float, src: c_float) -> c_float {\n86\t    if dest.is_nan() {\n87\t        quiet(dest)\n88\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n89\t        quiet(src)\n90\t    } else {\n91\t        dest / src\n92\t    }\n93\t}\n94\t\n95\t/// One `float` load, exactly like the `movss` gcc emits for `src[k]`.\n96\t///\n97\t/// This is deliberately *not* `*ptr` and *not* `ptr::read_volatile`:\n98\t///   * a plain load is sinkable, and LLVM does sink the `src[0]` load into the\n99\t///     `s != 0` branch (verified in the generated code). The C loads all three\n100\t///     elements unconditionally, in index order, *before* the `s == 0` test, so\n101\t///     with a plain load the two objects disagree when `src[0]` is unreadable\n102\t///     while `src[1..2]` are and `s == 0`;\n103\t///   * `ptr::read_volatile` fixes the order but adds an alignment/non-null\n104\t///     precondition (a hard `abort` in debug builds), whereas `movss` — and\n105\t///     therefore the C — happily accepts a misaligned pointer and faults with\n106\t///     `SIGSEGV`, not `SIGABRT`, on a null one.\n107\t///\n108\t/// The `asm!` has side effects (no `pure`), so it can be neither removed nor\n109\t/// sunk past the branch. No value is transformed, only the access itself is\n110\t/// pinned.\n111\t#[inline]\n112\t#[cfg(target_arch = \"x86_64\")]\n113\tunsafe fn load_f32(p: *const c_float) -> c_float {\n114\t    let out: c_float;\n115\t    core::arch::asm!(\n116\t        \"movss {out}, dword ptr [{p}]\",\n117\t        p = in(reg) p,\n118\t        out = out(xmm_reg) out,\n119\t        options(nostack, preserves_flags, readonly),\n120\t    );\n121\t    out\n122\t}\n123\t\n124\t/// One `float` store, exactly like the `movss` gcc emits for `dest[k]`.\n125\t/// Keeping the three stores separate and ordered matters for the case where a\n126\t/// later element is unwritable: the C commits `dest[0]`/`dest[1]` before it\n127\t/// faults on `dest[2]`.\n128\t#[inline]\n129\t#[cfg(target_arch = \"x86_64\")]\n130\tunsafe fn store_f32(p: *mut c_float, x: c_float) {\n131\t    core::arch::asm!(\n132\t        \"movss dword ptr [{p}], {x}\",\n133\t        p = in(reg) p,\n134\t        x = in(xmm_reg) x,\n135\t        options(nostack, preserves_flags),\n136\t    );\n137\t}\n138\t\n139\t/// Portable fallback. `[u8